     The following configuration settings are understood:

     sseett hhoossttaapp iinntteerrffaaccee _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e | {_i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_0, _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_1, _._._.}
             Specify the wireless interface running in Host AP mode.  This op‐
             tion could be omitted to use hostapd(8) to log received IAPP mes‐
             sages.  Multiple hostap interfaces may be specified as a comma-
             separated list, surrounded by curly braces.

     sseett hhoossttaapp mmooddee _m_o_d_e
             Specify the Host AP capture mode.  The supported modes are:

                   rraaddiioottaapp  Capture IEEE 802.11 frames with additional radio‐
                             tap headers.  They will provide optional but use‐
                             ful information like received frame signal lev‐
                             els.
                   ppccaapp      Capture plain IEEE 802.11 frames.

     sseett hhoossttaapp hhooppppeerr iinntteerrffaaccee _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e | {_i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_0, _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_1, _._._.}
             Enable a channel hopper on the selected wireless interface.  Mul‐
             tiple hostap interfaces may be specified as a comma-separated
             list, surrounded by curly braces.

     sseett hhoossttaapp hhooppppeerr ddeellaayy _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Set the delay in milliseconds for the channel hopper before hop‐
             ping to the next available channel.  The default value is 800
             milliseconds.

     sseett iiaapppp iinntteerrffaaccee _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             Specify the mandatory Inter-Access-Point (IAPP) interface.  It is
             important that the IAPP interface is on a trusted network because
             there is no authentication and an attacker could force disassoci‐
             ation of selected stations on all listening access points.

     sseett iiaapppp [aaddddrreessss | rroouuttee] rrooaammiinngg ttaabbllee <_t_a_b_l_e>
             Specify a table used for _I_P _R_o_a_m_i_n_g lookups of link layer address
             to IP address or subnet assignments.

     sseett iiaapppp hhaannddllee ssuubbttyyppee _s_u_b_t_y_p_e | {_s_u_b_t_y_p_e_0, _s_u_b_t_y_p_e_1, _._._.}
             Specify the IAPP subtypes to use:

                   [nnoott] aadddd nnoottiiffyy
                              Send and receive _A_D_D_._n_o_t_i_f_y messages.  This op‐
                              tion is enabled by default.
                   [nnoott] rraaddiioottaapp
                              Receive _r_a_d_i_o_t_a_p messages.  This option is en‐
                              abled by default.
                   [nnoott] [aaddddrreessss || rroouuttee] rrooaammiinngg
                              Enable dynamic roaming of IP addresses or
                              routes.  These options are disabled by default.

     sseett iiaapppp mmooddee _m_o_d_e
             Specify the IAPP mode.  The supported modes are:

                   mmuullttiiccaasstt [aaddddrreessss _i_p_v_4_a_d_d_r] [ppoorrtt _n_u_m_b_e_r] [ttttll _n_u_m_b_e_r]
                              Use multicast(4) frames.  A multicast time-to-
                              live (TTL) of 2 or higher is required to allow
                              multicast forwarding, for example for use with
                              mrouted(8).
