EEVVEENNTT RRUULLEESS
     Event rules provide a powerful way to trigger a certain action when re‐
     ceiving specified IEEE 802.11 frames on the _h_o_s_t_a_p _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e.  The rules
     are handled in sequential order, from first to last.  Rules are handled
     without a state: each rule is processed independently from the others and
     from any previous actions.  This behaviour is somewhat different to that
     of packet filter rules specified in pf.conf(5).

     All hostapd(8) event rules are single line statements beginning with the
     mandatory hhoossttaapp hhaannddllee keywords and optional rule options, interface,
     frame matching, a specified action, a limit, and a minimal rate:

           hhoossttaapp hhaannddllee [_o_p_t_i_o_n] [_i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e] [_f_r_a_m_e] [_a_c_t_i_o_n] [_l_i_m_i_t] [_r_a_t_e]

     Some rule statements support the optional keyword nnoott, also represented
     by the !! operator, for inverse matching.

     The optional parts are defined below.

   RRuullee OOppttiioonn
     The rule _o_p_t_i_o_n will modify the behaviour of handling the statement.
     There are two possible options, qquuiicckk and sskkiipp.  If either the keyword
     qquuiicckk or the keyword sskkiipp is specified, no further event rules will be
     handled for this frame after processing this rule successfully.  The key‐
     word sskkiipp additionally skips any further IAPP processing of the frame,
     which is normally done after handling the event rules.

   RRuullee IInntteerrffaaccee
     The rule _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e specifies the hostap interface the rule is matched on.
     The available interface list is specified by the global sseett hhoossttaapp
     iinntteerrffaaccee configuration setting.

           oonn [nnoott] _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e

     If not given, the event rule is matched on all available hostap inter‐
     faces.

   RRuullee FFrraammee
     The _f_r_a_m_e description specifies a mechanism to match IEEE 802.11 frames.

     aannyy     Match all frames.

     ffrraammee [_t_y_p_e] [_d_i_r] [_f_r_o_m] [_t_o] [_b_s_s_i_d] [_r_a_d_i_o_t_a_p]
             Apply rules to frames matching the given parameters.  The parame‐
             ters are explained below.

             The _t_y_p_e parameter specifies the frame type to match on.  The
             frame type may be specified in the following ways:

             ttyyppee aannyy
                     Match all frame types.

             ttyyppee [nnoott] ddaattaa
                     Match data frames.  Presence of the nnoott keyword negates
                     the match and will match all non-data frames.

             ttyyppee [nnoott] mmaannaaggeemmeenntt [[nnoott] _s_u_b_t_y_p_e]
                     Match management frames.  The _s_u_b_t_y_p_e argument may be
                     specified to optionally match management frames of the
                     given subtype.  The subtype match may be negated by spec‐
                     ifying the nnoott keyword.  See the _M_a_n_a_g_e_m_e_n_t _F_r_a_m_e
                     _S_u_b_t_y_p_e_s section below for available subtypes specifica‐
                     tions.

             The _d_i_r parameter specifies the direction the frame is being
             sent.  The direction may be specified in the following ways:

             ddiirr aannyy
                     Match all directions.

             ddiirr _f_r_a_m_e_d_i_r
                     Match frames with the given direction _f_r_a_m_e_d_i_r.  See the
                     _F_r_a_m_e _D_i_r_e_c_t_i_o_n_s section below for available direction
                     specifications.

             The _r_a_d_i_o_t_a_p rules allow parsing and matching of the extra infor‐
             mation reported by the radiotap header.  Support for the speci‐
             fied radiotap headers is optional and the specific parameters de‐
             pend on the radiotap elements reported by the wireless interface.
             Support for the radiotap data link type can be verified with the
             tcpdump(8) command.  These rules require hhoossttaapp mmooddee rraaddiioottaapp in
             the global configuration.

             ssiiggnnaall [ooppeerraattoorr] _p_e_r_c_e_n_t_a_g_e %%
                     Match the signal quality of the received frame.

             ffrreeqq [ooppeerraattoorr] _v_a_l_u_e (GGHHzz | MMHHzz)
                     Match the transmit rate of the received frame.

             ttxxrraattee [ooppeerraattoorr] _r_a_t_e MMbb
                     Match the frequency of the received frame, in Mbps.

             The radiotap rules support the following operators.  If omitted,
             the specified value will be checked if it is equal or not.

                   =       (equal)
                   !=      (not equal)
                   <       (less than)
                   <=      (less than or equal)
                   >       (greater than)
                   >=      (greater than or equal)

             The _f_r_o_m, _t_o, and _b_s_s_i_d parameters specify the IEEE 802.11 ad‐
             dress fields to match on.  They can be specified in the following
             ways:

             (ffrroomm | ttoo | bbssssiidd) aannyy
                     Allow all addresses for the specified address field.

             (ffrroomm | ttoo | bbssssiidd) [nnoott] <_t_a_b_l_e>
                     Allow allow addresses from the given _t_a_b_l_e (see _T_A_B_L_E_S
                     above) for the specified address field.

             (ffrroomm | ttoo | bbssssiidd) [nnoott] _l_l_a_d_d_r
                     Allow the given address _l_l_a_d_d_r for the specified address
                     field.

   RRuullee AAccttiioonn
     An optional _a_c_t_i_o_n is triggered if a received IEEE 802.11 frame matches
     the frame description.  The following actions are supported:

     wwiitthh ffrraammee _t_y_p_e [_d_i_r] _f_r_o_m _t_o _b_s_s_i_d
             Send an arbitrary constructed frame to the wireless network.  The
             arguments are as follows.

             The _t_y_p_e describes the IEEE 802.11 frame type to send, specified
             in the frame control header.  The following frames types are sup‐
             ported at present:

             ttyyppee ddaattaa
                     Send a data frame.  This is normally used to encapsulate
                     ordinary IEEE 802.3 frames into IEEE 802.11 wireless
                     frames.

             ttyyppee mmaannaaggeemmeenntt _s_u_b_t_y_p_e
                     Send a management frame with the specified subtype.  Man‐
                     agement frames are used to control states and to find ac‐
                     cess points and IBSS nodes in IEEE 802.11 networks.  See
                     the _M_a_n_a_g_e_m_e_n_t _F_r_a_m_e _S_u_b_t_y_p_e_s section below for available
                     subtypes specifications.

             The _d_i_r describes the direction the IEEE 802.11 frame will be
             sent.  It has the following syntax:

                   ddiirr _f_r_a_m_e_d_i_r

             See the _F_r_a_m_e _D_i_r_e_c_t_i_o_n_s section below for available direction
             specifications.

             The _f_r_o_m, _t_o, and _b_s_s_i_d arguments specify the link layer address
             fields used in IEEE 802.11 frames.  All address fields are manda‐
             tory in the frame action.  The optional fourth address field used
             by wireless distribution systems (WDS) is currently not sup‐
             ported.  Each argument is specified by a keyword of the same name
             (ffrroomm, ttoo, or bbssssiidd) followed by one of the following address
             specifications:

             _l_l_a_d_d_r    Specify the link layer addresses used in the IEEE
                       802.11 frame address field.  The link layer address
                       ‘ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff’ is the IEEE 802.11 broadcast ad‐
                       dress.

             &_r_e_f_a_d_d_r  Fill in a link layer address from the previously
                       matched IEEE 802.11 frame.  &&ffrroomm will use the source
                       link layer address; &&ttoo the destination link layer ad‐
                       dress; and &&bbssssiidd the BSSID link layer address of the
                       previously matched frame.

             rraannddoomm    Use a random link layer address in the specified IEEE
                       802.11 frame address field.  Multicast and broadcast
                       link layer addresses will be skipped.

     wwiitthh iiaapppp ttyyppee _i_a_p_p_-_t_y_p_e
             Send a hostapd(8) specific IAPP frame with a raw IEEE 802.11
             packet dump of the received frame to the wired network.  The only
             supported _i_a_p_p_-_t_y_p_e is rraaddiioottaapp.

     wwiitthh lloogg [vveerrbboossee]
             Write informational messages to the local system log (see
             syslogd(8)) or standard error.  If the _R_u_l_e _R_a_t_e has been speci‐
             fied, log will print the actual rate.

     nnooddee aadddd | ddeelleettee _l_l_a_d_d_r
             Add or remove the specified node from the internal kernel node
             table.

     rreesseenndd  Resend the received IEEE 802.11 frame.

   RRuullee LLiimmiitt
     It is possible to limit handling of specific rules with the lliimmiitt key‐
     word:

           lliimmiitt _n_u_m_b_e_r sseecc | uusseecc

     In some cases it is absolutely necessary to use limited matching to pro‐
     tect hostapd(8) against excessive flooding with IEEE 802.11 frames.  For
     example, beacon frames will be normally received every 100 ms.

   RRuullee RRaattee
     It is possible to tell hostapd(8) to trigger the action only after a spe‐
     cific rraattee of matched frames.

           rraattee _n_u_m_b_e_r _/ _n_u_m_b_e_r sseecc

     This will help to detect excessive flooding of IEEE 802.11 frames.  For
     example, de-auth flooding is a denial of service (DoS) attack against
     IEEE 802.11 wireless networks.

   MMaannaaggeemmeenntt FFrraammee SSuubbttyyppeess
     The _s_u_b_t_y_p_e describes the IEEE 802.11 frame subtype, specified in the
     frame control header.  The choice of subtypes depends on the used frame
     type.  hostapd(8) currently only supports management frame subtypes.
     Most frame subtypes require an additional subtype-specific header in the
     frame body, but currently only the ddeeaauutthh and ddiissaassssoocc reason codes are
     supported:

        ssuubbttyyppee bbeeaaccoonn
        A beacon frame.  Wireless access points and devices running in _i_b_s_s
        master or _h_o_s_t_a_p mode continuously send beacon frames to indicate
        their presence, traffic load, and capabilities.

        ssuubbttyyppee ddeeaauutthh [_r_e_a_s_o_n]
        A deauthentication frame with an optional reason code.  Deauthenti‐
        cated stations will lose any IEEE 802.11 operational state.

        ssuubbttyyppee ddiissaassssoocc [_r_e_a_s_o_n]
        A disassociation frame with an optional reason code.

        ssuubbttyyppee aassssoocc rreeqquueesstt
        An association request frame.

        ssuubbttyyppee aassssoocc rreessppoonnssee
        An association response frame.

        ssuubbttyyppee aattiimm
        An announcement traffic indication message (ATIM frame).

        ssuubbttyyppee aauutthh [ooppeenn rreeqquueesstt | rreessppoonnssee]
        An authentication frame.

        ssuubbttyyppee pprroobbee rreeqquueesstt
        A probe request frame.  Probe requests are used to probe for access
        points and IBSS nodes.

        ssuubbttyyppee pprroobbee rreessppoonnssee
        A probe response frame.

        ssuubbttyyppee rreeaassssoocc rreeqquueesstt
        A re-association request frame.

        ssuubbttyyppee rreeaassssoocc rreessppoonnssee
        A re-association response frame.

     The _r_e_a_s_o_n defines a descriptive reason for the actual _d_e_a_u_t_h_e_n_t_i_c_a_t_i_o_n
     or _d_i_s_a_s_s_o_c_i_a_t_i_o_n of a station:

